<h2>Indian fresh vegetables for export</h2>
<p>India's vegetable export trade splits into two quite different businesses. The first is volume commodity trade — onion above all, plus potato and garlic — moving in mesh bags to Bangladesh, Malaysia, Sri Lanka, Nepal and the Gulf, where price and reliability of supply decide the order. The second is specialty produce — drumstick (moringa), okra, green chilli, curry leaf and similar — shipping smaller volumes at higher value, largely into markets with significant South Asian diaspora demand.</p>

<p>We handle both. What differs is the packing, the cold-chain requirement and how tightly you need to specify grade.</p>

<h2>Export policy is a live consideration on vegetables</h2>
<p>This matters more for Indian vegetables than for most product categories we handle. The Indian government actively manages exports of staple vegetables — onion especially — to protect domestic supply and consumer prices, using minimum export prices, export duties and, at times, outright bans.</p>
<p>We track the current position and confirm applicable terms when your order is placed. What we won't do is quote you a fixed price months ahead as though policy were settled. If you're planning a recurring vegetable programme, build that variability into your plan and talk to us about structuring around it. See our <a href="/products/fresh-vegetables/red-onion/">red onion page</a> for a fuller explanation of how this works in practice.</p>

<h2>Packing and airflow</h2>
<p>The single most common cause of vegetable shipments arriving in poor condition is packaging that traps moisture. Onion, potato and garlic need airflow — mesh or net bags, ventilated containers, and properly cured stock going in. Sealed or over-tight packing causes rot regardless of transit time. For delicate items, ventilated cartons with the right reefer setting do the equivalent job.</p>

<h2>Specify grade explicitly</h2>
<p>"Export quality" means nothing as a specification. For onion, specify size band (40–60mm, 50–70mm, 60–80mm). For potato, specify size and variety. For chilli, specify length, colour and pungency expectations. Vague specifications are the root of most quality disputes, and they're avoidable in a single email at the enquiry stage.</p>

